    "reducedMotion": "The loop never starts. A short frozen swipe — nine samples with descending life and a plausible velocity — is baked into the ring and one frame is drawn, so the panel shows what the effect produces rather than a flat dimmed image. Coarse pointers get the same still, for the same reason. The listeners stay attached but their target is never consumed, so moving the pointer changes nothing.",
    "cleanup": "Removes the three pointer listeners from the canvas, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a dead renderer, then `disposeRenderer(renderer)` — which force-loses the context, not just `dispose()`, since Chromium keeps roughly sixteen per process and drops the oldest — followed by the geometry, the material and the 1x1 placeholder texture, and finally removes the canvas from the DOM. The decoded image texture is disposed in a separate unmount effect, and an in-flight decode is cancelled by a flag so its callback disposes the result instead of binding it.",
    "acceptance": "At rest the image is visible but dimmed and nearly grey. Drag across it and full colour appears in a trail of hard-edged square blocks that widens and fades behind the pointer, with the blocks smeared along the direction of travel — more on a flick, less on a slow drag. The untouched area contains no stray lit pixels anywhere. Stop moving and the reveal stays alive under the cursor instead of dying. Leave the panel and the trail finishes ageing out where it stands. The follow lag is identical on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display. Point at a `src` that 404s: the panel shows a banded stand-in field, the trail still works, and the console carries no exception. Resize wide and the image stays cover-cropped with no stretching, and the trail discs stay round.",
