    "reducedMotion": "The component reads `useReducedMotion` itself and freezes `uTime` at 0 rather than stopping the render. The fog, the vignette, the grid and the cursor glow are all still drawn — none of them depends on time — so what is lost is exactly the drift and nothing else. With time constant the image is a pure function of the pointer, so the loop skips any frame in which the pointer has not moved by more than 0.0002, and a still page costs nothing beyond the empty rAF callback.",
    "cleanup": "Nulls the stage ref, disposes the triangle geometry and the ShaderMaterial, then calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed drawing buffer, and removes the canvas from the DOM. The canvas is three's own, created inside the effect and appended per effect run, so a StrictMode remount builds a new context instead of reattaching one that was just force-lost. The pointer listener belongs to `useSmoothPointer` and is removed with it; sizing, visibility and the frame loop are all shared subscriptions that release themselves.",
    "acceptance": "A soft blue fog drifting behind a faint grid, with the grid brightening into a pool under the cursor that trails a third of a second behind it. Open DevTools and the canvas element reports a backing store of 0.6 of its CSS size — the technique is verifiable, not merely claimed — while the image shows no blockiness and the grid lines stay continuous rather than dashed. Resize the window slowly: the grid keeps its 44 px spacing against the layout and never crawls. With reduced motion the fog stops drifting and everything else, cursor glow included, still works.",
