    "reducedMotion": "The stack holds still — the phase is integrated from delta time, so freezing it is a matter of adding nothing — and with nothing marking the frame dirty the renderer draws once per build and then idles. The demonstration survives the loss of motion because it was never really about the motion: the build counter still climbs under `activity` and stays pinned under `unmount`, and the rebuild cost in milliseconds is still charged on every show. Only the resumed rotation angle stops being informative, since both arms sit at zero degrees.",
    "cleanup": "The Effect removes each slab and disposes its material, disposes the one shared geometry, calls `disposeRenderer` — which stops the loop, disposes the renderer and calls `forceContextLoss()` — and removes the canvas it appended. This runs on unmount, on a StrictMode double-invoke, and on every Activity hide, so it is exercised constantly rather than once. `useRaf` unsubscribes and the shared loop parks when its last subscriber leaves; the `ResizeObserver` unobserves with its hook. A separate Effect reports the scene as gone on teardown so the readout does not freeze on a stale row.",
    "acceptance": "On `activity`: press hide, wait, press show. The stack picks up at the angle the readout froze at, `builds` has gone up by one, and the rebuild cost is a real number of milliseconds. On `unmount`: the same sequence returns the stack to zero degrees and `builds` never moves. Hide on either arm and the canvas is gone from the DOM inspector under `unmount` but present with `display: none` under `activity`. Neither arm accumulates WebGL contexts — toggle twenty times and the other previews on the page stay drawn.",
