    "reducedMotion": "The loop never starts. One frame is drawn with the text rect fully avoided and the pointer parked off to one side at full presence, so the still shows exactly the shape a moving version produces — the drift is the only thing lost. Re-measuring after a font swap redraws that single frame rather than starting a loop. The listeners stay attached but their target is never consumed.",
    "cleanup": "Removes the three pointer listeners from the canvas, cancels the pending `document.fonts.ready` callback with a flag, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a dead renderer, then `geometry.remove()` (deletes the VAOs and every buffer), `program.remove()` (deletes the shaders and the program), `loseContext(gl)`, zeroes the canvas dimensions and removes it from the DOM. ogl has no `dispose()`, and dropping the context is the part that matters — the per-process budget is about sixteen, and exceeding it kills the oldest, which belongs to someone else.",
    "acceptance": "The headline sits in a clear zone whose shape is a squircle, not a circle and not a rectangle: the gap above and below the line is the same as the gap at its ends, and the corners are rounded without being round. A brighter rim traces that shape. Resize the container until the headline rewraps to two lines — the clear zone re-fits within a frame, with no visible sweep from the old rect. Move the pointer in and a second, softer hole follows it with a short even lag that is identical on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display; the hole stays circular on a wide container and does not disappear when the pointer crosses the headline. No particle sits inside the clear zone and none pile into a single dense outline. Reload twice: the field is identical. With reduced motion the panel is one composed still with the same shape.",
